ch\nDASIV SmartState Center\nAreas of Research\nPublications\nEvents\nSpring School\nPrevious Schools\nSpring School 2021\nSpring School 2019\nSpring School 2018\nWorkshop on Variationally Stable Neural Networks\nFaculty Members\nRTG: Foundation of Data Science\nResearch Experience for Undergraduates\nFaculty Members\nSummer School on Mathematical Foundation of Data Science\nThe Historic IMI\nResearch\nPreprint Series\nBeyond the Classroom\nColloquia And Seminars\nDepartment Colloquia\nGraduate Colloquium\nDiscrete and Combinatorics Seminars\nRTG Data Science Seminars\nQuantum Information & Analysis Seminars\nApplied and Computational Mathematics Seminars\nApplied and Computational Student Seminars\nAlgebraic Geometry, Commutative Algebra and Number Theory Seminar\nFRG on Variationally Stable Neural Networks\nHarmonic Analysis and PDE Seminar\nWorkshops\n2023 Workshop on Scientific Computing and Large Data\n2019 Workshop on Nonlinear Approximation\nFor the Community\nHigh School Math Contest\nRegistration\nPrevious Contests\n2023: 36th High School Math Contest\n2022: 35th High School Math Contest\nResults\nPictures\n2020: 34th High School Math Contest\nGeneral Information\nParticipants\nResults\nSolutions\nGallery\n2019: 33rd High School Math Contest\n2018: 32nd High School Math Contest\n2018: 32nd High School Math Contest\n2017: 31st High School Math Contest\nContest Overview\nGeneral Information\nOverall Results 2017\nParticipant List 2017\nScore Ranking 2017\nTeam Ranking 2017\nQuestion Statistics 2017\n2016: 30th High School Math Contest\nGeneral Information 2016\nContest Overview\nOverall Results 2016\nParticipant List 2016\nTeam Ranking 2016\nQuestion Statistics 2016\n2015: 29th High School Math Contest\nGeneral Information\nContest Overview\nParticipant List 2015\nOverall Results 2015\nScore Ranking 2015\nTeam Ranking 2015\nQuestion Statistics 2015\n2014: 28th High School Math Contest\nGeneral Information\nContest Overview\nParticipant List 2014\nOverall Results 2014\nScore Ranking 2014\nTeam Ranking 2014\nResult Statistics 2014\n2013: 27th High School Math Contest\nGeneral Information\nContest Overview\nMaps and Parking\nParticipant List 2013\nOverall Results 2013\nTeam Ranking 2013\nScore Ranking 2013\nQuestion Statistics 2013\n2025: 39th High School Math Contest\nAP Calculus Practice Test\nStudy Away\nPfF Plus\nPeer Mentor Program\nPast Mentors\nCollaborators\nAssociation for Women in Mathematics\nOur People\nAlumni\nGive\nMy Mathematics\nTools & Resources\nCalendar\nMap\nDirectory\nApply\nGive\nStudent Gateway\nFaculty & Staff Gateway\nParents & Families Gateway\nAlumni Gateway\nDepartment of Mathematics\nSC.edu\nStudy\nColleges and Schools\nArts and Sciences\nDepartment of Mathematics\nApply\nGraduate Applicants\nDepartment of Mathematics\nAbout\nStudy\nApply\nResearch\nBeyond the Classroom\nOur People\nAlumni\nGive\nMy Mathematics\nGraduate Applicants\nAwards and Scholarships\nGraduate Applicants\nAll graduate students apply through the university's graduate school. Please contact\nus with any questions you have specific to our department.\n```html\nDeadline\nThe deadline for Fall 2027 admission is February 28, 2027.\nBasic Application Parts\nOfficial Transcripts from all colleges and universities attended for course credit\nTest Scores\nApplicants whose native language is not English must supply TOEFL or IELTS scores\nTwo or more Letters of Recommendation\nA Statement of Purpose (your mathematics goals, interests, experiences, etc.)\nSupplemental Materials (as applicable).\nExamples include CV/resume, papers, preprints, etc.\nTOEFL Requirements\nThe University requires an Internet based TOEFL score of 80 or better (or equivalent\naccording to concordance tables) for admission. For teaching assistantship, our Department\nrequires an Internet based TOEFL score of 100 or better. If you completed a degree\nfrom an institution of higher education in the US or Canada, the university waives\nthe TOEFL requirement.\nAssistantships\nWe expect to award 10\u201313 assistantships in Fall 2027. The starting annual stipend\nfor new assistantships is $21,000 ($10,500 per semester). New assistantships will\nalso get full tuition abatement. Subject to annual performance reviews of scholarship\nand teaching, these assistantships are renewable for up to 5 years.\nIn the last 10 years, we have been able to offer summer stipends to all students who\nprepare for the qualifying exams in the summer after their first year, and to most\nstudents who prepare for the comprehensive exams in the summer after the second year.\nSummer stipend amounts vary depending on the teaching assignment. Many students in\nsummers beyond their third year find support from individual faculty member grants\nor from external sources such as internships or other employment outside USC.\nWhen comparing financial packages, keep in mind that the cost of living in Columbia,\nSC is very low.\nFellowships\nStrong applicants are also eligible for two types of Fellowships.\nPresidential Fellowship: $12,000/year for 4 years.\nCollege of Arts Sciences Fellowship: $4,000/year for 4 years.\nTeaching responsibilities.\nAll graduate student assistants are expected to teach, except for those supported\nby faculty grants.\nFirst-year, first-semester students will be teaching assistants for Calculus I or\nCalculus II. In the second semester of the first year and the first semester of the\nsecond year, students take 1-credit courses on math pedagogy (MATH 791 and 792) under\nthe instruction of Dr. Sean Yee.\nStarting in the second year, students who completed, or are taking, Dr. Yee's courses\nare eligible to be instructor of record for the following courses:\nMATH 111 (College Algebra),\nMATH115 (Precalculus),\nMATH122 (Business Calculus),\nMATH170 (Finite Mathematics),\nMATH174 (Discrete Math for Computer Science).\nOn certain occasions, usually in Spring, experienced student instructors can be instructor\nof record for\nMATH141 (Calculus I),\nMATH142 (Calculus II),\nMATH241 (Calculus III),\nMATH242 (Elementary Differential Equations)\nMATH374 (Discrete Math).\nOutcomes:\nIn recent years, our Ph.D. graduates have taken postdoctoral appointments at Arizona\nState, U. Delaware, Georgia Tech, Harvard, Max Planck, Penn St., Queens U., UC-San\nDiego, U. Southern California, Texas A&M, U. Utah, and Wake Forest. Our graduates\nhave also taken jobs with Ernst & Young, Microsoft, Google, NSA, NASA, Texas Instruments,\nand Wells Fargo.\n```\nApply\nGraduate School\nWhether you are seeking a master's, doctorate or postgraduate certificate, we make\nyour application process as simple as possible.\nApply Now\nDepartment of Mathematics\nLocation\nContact\nGive\nCalendar\nSocial Media\nColleges & Schools\nArts and Sciences\nBusiness\nEducation\nEngineering and Computing\nThe Graduate School\nHospitality, Retail and Sport Management\nInformation and Communications\nLaw\nMedicine\nMedicine (Greenville)\nMusic\nNursing\nPharmacy\nPublic Health\nSocial Work\nSouth Carolina Honors College\nPalmetto College\nOffices & Divisions\nEmployment\nUndergraduate Admissions\nFinancial Aid and Scholarships\nBursar (fee payment)\nVeterans and Military Services\nDisability Resources\nAccess and Opportunity\nHuman Resources\nLaw Enforcement and Safety\nUniversity Libraries\nAll Offices and Divisions\nTools\nCarolina Alert\nCampus Email\nmy.sc.edu\nBlackboard\nCampus Safety and Wellness\nPeopleSoft Finance\n\u00a9\nUniversity of South Carolina\nPrivacy\nStudent Consumer Information\nStudent Consumer Information\nTransparency Initiative\nCivil Rights and Title IX\nDigital Accessibility\nContact\nChallenge the conventional. Create the exceptional. No Limits.\n\u00a9", "links": [["Skip to Content", "https://sc.edu/study/colleges_schools/artsandsciences/mathematics/apply/graduate_applicants/#mainContent"], ["University of South Carolina Home", "https://sc.edu"], ["STUDENTS", "https://www.sc.edu/students/"], ["FACULTY & STAFF", "https://